{{also|Planet|planèt|plånet}}
==English==
[[File:Solar_system.jpg|thumb|'''Planets''' of the Solar System]]

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|planete}}, from {{der|en|fro|planete}}, from {{der|en|la|planeta}}, {{m|la|planetes}}, from {{der|en|grc|πλανήτης|t=wanderer}} ({{ellipsis of|grc|πλάνητες ἀστέρες|t=wandering stars|nocap=1|nocat=1}}), from {{der|en|grc|πλανάω|t=wander about, stray}}, of unknown origin. Cognate with {{cog|la|pālor|t=wander about, stray}}, {{cog|non|flana|t=to rush about}}, and {{cog|no|flanta|t=to wander about}}. More at {{l|en|flaunt}}.

Perhaps it is from a nasalized form of {{der|en|ine-pro|*pleh₂-|t=flat, broad}} on the notion of "spread out", "but the semantics are highly problematic", according to Beekes, who notes the similarity of meaning to {{m|grc|πλάζω|t=to make devious, repel, dissuade from the right path, bewilder}}, but adds, "it is hard to think of a formal connection".

So called because they have apparent motion, unlike the "fixed" stars. Originally including also the moon and sun but not the Earth; modern scientific sense of "world that orbits a star" is from 1630s in English. The Greek word is an enlarged form of {{m|grc|πλάνης|t=who wanders around, wanderer}}, also "wandering star, planet", in medicine "unstable temperature."

